ts each of  which were less than two months in  advance so I guess the first lesson the  idea that if you have a vision and the  vision is clear enough you may still  have to spend a lot of work just getting  you know a lot of it's the work just  getting the assays getting the  procedures right but then the next thing  this is thing that I really wanted to  talk about next and the next number  actually and we were we did actually  start out with trying to make 40,000  lines well as you'll see we only really  carried about 27,000 of them through to  the third generation but by the time we  gotten to that generation began to get  our first results we got it a important  first important really result that we'd  used enough mutagenesis that in the  lines that we were looking at we had  about and these 27,000 lines we had  about 18,000 different mutations and so  that meant that our mutagenesis  procedures were work everything was  working fine our interest though these  mutations kill the fight at any time  during the during its life cycle and  when we were really interested in what  genes that control the way embryos  developed and this is the point actually  where I think we were extraordinarily  lucky because what we had to do was now  from all these 18,000 eight of these  different lines we had to collect mutant  embryos we had to collect embryos look  at the embryos that died and decide  whether the gene that was mutated in  those lines actually killed the embryos  and killed him in an interesting way and  the great result thing that we couldn't  have anticipated was that of the 18,000  different lethal mutations only a tiny  tiny fraction actually changed  homozygous embryos in a way that was  really meaningful and we most of the  time the homozygous mutant embryos  hatched and crawled away it didn't have  to be that way and what would have been  really really horrible for us and  thinking back to it is that if every one  of those 18,000 lines we picked out  looke